# 17. Denial of Service

AEGIS must consider attacks or failures that prevent users from accessing:

```text
dashboard
investigations
alerts
telemetry
intelligence
authentication
APIs
```

Availability is a safety concern when users depend on the system during time-sensitive operations.

---

# 18. Elevation of Privilege

A user or compromised service must not gain permissions beyond its assigned authority.

Controls:

```text
RBAC
least privilege
server-side authorization
permission checks
privileged access monitoring
session controls
```

---

# 19. Trust Boundary: User → Frontend

Threats include:

```text
malicious input
session theft
client manipulation
browser compromise
unauthorized UI actions
```

The frontend must never be treated as a trusted security boundary.

---

# 20. Trust Boundary: Frontend → API

All security-critical validation must occur server-side.

The API must independently verify:

```text
identity
authorization
input validity
resource access
operation permission
```

Never trust the frontend to enforce security.

---

# 21. Trust Boundary: API → Services

Internal services must also authenticate and authorize requests where required.

Internal traffic must not automatically be considered trusted.

---

# 22. Trust Boundary: Services → Database

Services should access only the data required for their responsibilities.

Controls:

```text
least privilege
service identities
parameterized queries
input validation
database auditing
```

---

# 23. Trust Boundary: AEGIS → External Systems

External systems must be treated as potentially unreliable.

Possible conditions:

```text
unavailable
incorrect
delayed
duplicated
malformed
compromised
incompatible
```

AEGIS must validate incoming data.

---

# 24. Sensor Spoofing Risk

AEGIS may receive operational telemetry from external sources.

The architecture must account for:

```text
false readings
missing readings
duplicate readings
delayed readings
unexpected readings
source identity failure
```

AEGIS should never assume that a sensor is truthful simply because it is registered.

---

# 25. Source Trust

A telemetry source should have an identifiable trust and quality state.

Conceptually:

```text
TRUSTED
DEGRADED
UNVERIFIED
UNAVAILABLE
REVOKED
```

The exact trust model must be defined with stakeholders and security requirements.

---

# 26. Data Integrity Risk

Incorrect data can be more dangerous than unavailable data.

Therefore AEGIS must detect:

```text
unexpected changes
schema violations
duplicate records
invalid values
source inconsistencies
timestamp anomalies
```

---

# 27. Data Poisoning Risk

AI and analytical systems may be affected by deliberately or accidentally corrupted input data.

AEGIS should mitigate through:

```text
source validation
quality monitoring
provenance
anomaly detection
data review
controlled training data
model governance
```

