# 28. AI Threat Surface

The AI intelligence layer introduces additional risks:

```text
hallucination
prompt injection
malicious retrieved content
data poisoning
model drift
model misuse
overconfidence
automation bias
incorrect summarization
incorrect classification
```

---

# 29. AI Output Integrity

AI output must not automatically become trusted system state.

The architecture should preserve:

```text
generated
reviewed
approved
rejected
superseded
```

states where applicable.

---

# 30. Prompt Injection

If AEGIS uses generative AI with external or user-provided content, malicious instructions embedded inside retrieved content may attempt to manipulate the model.

Controls include:

```text
trusted retrieval boundaries
input separation
instruction hierarchy
output validation
tool permission restrictions
human review
```

---

# 31. AI Tool Access

AI systems should receive only the minimum tools and permissions required.

For example:

```text
AI may read approved evidence
```

does not imply:

```text
AI may modify evidence
```

and:

```text
AI may recommend an action
```

does not imply:

```text
AI may execute the action
```

---

# 32. AI Permission Boundary

The architecture should enforce:

```text
AI
 ↓
READ
 ↓
ANALYZE
 ↓
EXPLAIN
 ↓
RECOMMEND
```

while consequential operations remain behind explicit authorization.

---

# 33. AI Hallucination Risk

The system must assume that generative AI can produce unsupported statements.

Controls:

```text
retrieval grounding
structured outputs
source attribution
confidence
uncertainty
validation
human review
restricted action permissions
```

---

# 34. False Confidence

A highly polished interface can create excessive trust.

AEGIS must therefore avoid:

```text
false precision
unexplained scores
unqualified certainty
```

The UI should make uncertainty visible.

---

# 35. Automation Bias

Users may accept system recommendations simply because they came from AEGIS.

Mitigations:

```text
show evidence
show uncertainty
show alternatives where appropriate
require human confirmation
record decisions
```

---

# 36. Confirmation Bias

The system must avoid presenting only evidence supporting an initial hypothesis.

Where appropriate, users should be able to inspect:

```text
supporting evidence
conflicting evidence
unknowns
alternative interpretations
```

---

# 37. Premature Closure

AEGIS should not force an investigation into a definitive classification when evidence remains incomplete.

Possible state:

```text
INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE
```

must be valid.

---

# 38. Alert Manipulation

Attackers or faulty systems could attempt to create:

```text
false alerts
alert floods
alert suppression
```

Controls include:

```text
source validation
rate controls
correlation
deduplication
alert prioritization
audit logging
```

---

# 39. Alert Suppression Risk

Alert suppression can become dangerous if legitimate events are hidden.

Therefore suppression should be:

```text
explicit
authorized
auditable
time-bounded where appropriate
reversible
```

---

# 40. Notification Failure

A notification system can fail even when the underlying AEGIS system is functioning.

Therefore:

```text
alert created
≠
notification successfully delivered
```

These states must remain separate.

---

# 41. Decision Support Risk

AEGIS may produce a technically correct recommendation that is inappropriate because of missing context.

Therefore recommendations should expose:

```text
evidence
constraints
uncertainty
required authority
```

---

# 42. Human Decision Risk

Users can:

```text
misunderstand
misread
ignore
over-trust
incorrectly modify
incorrectly authorize
```

