# 48. Configuration Risk

A secure architecture can become insecure through configuration.

Examples:

```text
incorrect permissions
disabled logging
wrong environment variables
exposed service
incorrect thresholds
incorrect integrations
```

Configuration changes must be governed.

---

# 49. Secrets Management

Secrets must not be stored in:

```text
source code
frontend bundles
logs
public repositories
plain-text configuration
```

Use an appropriate secrets-management mechanism in production.

---

# 50. Environment Separation

AEGIS should separate:

```text
DEVELOPMENT
TEST
STAGING
PRODUCTION
```

Production credentials and data must not casually flow into development environments.

---

# 51. Development Data

Development environments should use:

```text
synthetic data
approved anonymized data
safe fixtures
```

where possible.

---

# 52. Production Data Protection

Production operational information must have explicit controls for:

```text
access
storage
transmission
retention
backup
deletion
```

---

# 53. Backup Risk

Backups can become an attack target.

Controls include:

```text
access control
encryption
backup integrity checks
retention policy
restore testing
```

---

# 54. Recovery Risk

A backup that has never been successfully restored is not a proven recovery mechanism.

AEGIS must eventually test:

```text
backup
 ↓
restore
 ↓
validation
```

---

# 55. Availability Risk

AEGIS should identify critical dependencies.

Conceptually:

```text
Authentication
Database
API
Telemetry
Intelligence
Notification
```

Each should have a defined failure mode.

---

# 56. Graceful Degradation

AEGIS should degrade rather than collapse when non-critical services fail.

Example:

```text
AI unavailable
↓
manual assessment remains available
```

---

# 57. Fail-Safe Principle

When uncertainty is high, AEGIS should avoid silently performing irreversible consequential operations.

Preferred:

```text
UNCERTAIN
↓
SURFACE UNCERTAINTY
↓
REQUEST HUMAN REVIEW
```

---

# 58. Fail-Closed vs Fail-Open

Security-sensitive permissions should generally fail closed.

Example:

```text
Authorization unavailable
↓
Do not grant privileged access
```

Operational visibility may require different degraded behavior.

The appropriate mode must be defined per subsystem.

---

# 59. Risk of Stale Data

A stale dashboard can create false operational confidence.

Therefore:

```text
fresh
aging
stale
unknown
```

must be distinguishable.

This directly inherits the requirement from Document 12.

---

# 60. Risk of Missing Data

Missing data must never automatically become:

```text
NORMAL
```

Instead:

```text
UNKNOWN
```

or an appropriate degraded state.

---

# 61. Risk of Conflicting Sources

When sources disagree:

```text
Source A → condition X

Source B → condition Y
```

AEGIS should preserve the conflict.

It must not silently select one without an established methodology.

---

# 62. Risk of Model Drift

Model performance can degrade because the operating environment changes.

Controls:

```text
performance monitoring
drift monitoring
validation
versioning
rollback
```

---

# 63. Risk of Baseline Drift

A changing environment may cause old baselines to become inappropriate.

AEGIS should support:

```text
baseline version
baseline review
baseline change history
```

---

# 64. Risk of Threshold Misconfiguration

Thresholds may be changed incorrectly.

Controls:

```text
authorization
validation
versioning
change audit
testing
rollback
```

---

# 65. Risk of Silent Architecture Drift

As the project evolves, implementation may diverge from architecture.

AEGIS must treat documentation as an architectural contract.

If implementation differs:

```text
STOP
↓
identify deviation
↓
review
↓
approve or reject
↓
update source of truth
```

