# 77. Common-Cause Failure

Multiple components may depend on the same underlying service.

Example:

```text
Frontend
API
Intelligence
Notifications
```

all depend on:

```text
Database
```

The database becomes a common point of failure.

Architecture must identify such dependencies.

---

# 78. Single Points of Failure

AEGIS should identify:

```text
single database
single authentication service
single notification provider
single integration
single AI provider
single network path
```

and determine appropriate resilience strategies.

---

# 79. Dependency Concentration

Reliance on one external provider can create:

```text
availability risk
cost risk
vendor lock-in
security risk
operational dependency
```

This should be considered during infrastructure design.

---

# 80. Human Factors Risk

AEGIS must account for:

```text
cognitive overload
alert fatigue
ambiguity
poor information hierarchy
misleading visualization
unclear status
false confidence
workflow interruption
```

---

# 81. Clinical Authority Principle

The visual system must communicate authority without communicating false certainty.

The interface should feel:

```text
precise
calm
evidence-driven
professional
controlled
```

not:

```text
dramatic
gamified
alarmist
sensational
```

This preserves the Clinical Authority principle established in the Master Architecture.

---

# 82. Risky Visualization

Avoid visualizations that exaggerate risk through:

```text
unexplained giant numbers
constant flashing
decorative threat effects
ambiguous color semantics
animations that obscure state
```

Visual emphasis must correspond to meaningful operational priority.

---

# 83. Accessibility Risk

Critical information must not depend solely on color.

For example:

```text
amber = alert
```

should be accompanied by:

```text
label
icon
status
text
```

where appropriate.

---

# 84. Localization Risk

If AEGIS eventually operates across regions:

```text
timezone
date format
measurement units
language
regulatory context
```

must be handled explicitly.

---

# 85. Risk Communication

The system should communicate risk using structured language.

Example:

```text
Assessment:
Moderate concern

Confidence:
Moderate

Uncertainty:
Recent source data unavailable

Reason:
Observed deviation from established baseline
```

This is preferable to:

```text
HIGH THREAT!!!
```

without context.

---

# 86. Security vs Usability

Security controls must not unnecessarily prevent authorized users from performing critical work.

AEGIS should optimize:

```text
strong security
+
usable workflows
```

rather than treating them as opposites.

---

# 87. Zero Trust Principle

AEGIS should not automatically trust:

```text
user
device
service
network
integration
data source
```

based solely on location or network position.

Trust should be continuously evaluated where appropriate.

---

# 88. Least Privilege

Every identity should receive only the permissions required.

This applies to:

```text
users
services
AI agents
integrations
automation
administrators
```

---

# 89. AI Least Privilege

AI systems should have particularly constrained permissions.

For example:

```text
read evidence
=
not
modify evidence

generate recommendation
=
not
execute response
```

